<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>A line joining points on two sides</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>In triangle OAB, \(\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}\) and \(\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}\). P is on OA with \(OP : PA = {@m@} : {@n@}\), and Q is on OB with \(OQ : QB = {@m@} : {@n@}\).</p><div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="Triangle OAB with P on OA and Q on OB, joined by a dashed line PQ. Not to scale.">
[[jsxgraph width="420px" height="300px"]]
(function() {
  var b = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {boundingbox: [-0.6, 5.6, 8.6, -0.8], axis: false, grid: false, showNavigation: false, showCopyright: false, pan: {enabled: false}, zoom: {enabled: false}});
  var lo = {strokeColor: '#000000', strokeWidth: 2, fixed: true, highlight: false};
  var po = {fixed: true, highlight: false, size: 2, fillColor: '#000000', strokeColor: '#000000', withLabel: false};
  var tx = {fixed: true, highlight: false, useMathJax: false, fontSize: 15};
  // triangle OAB, fixed positions (not to scale)
  var O = [0, 0], A = [7.5, 0.6], B = [2.6, 4.8];
  b.create('segment', [O, A], lo); b.create('segment', [O, B], lo); b.create('segment', [A, B], lo);
  b.create('text', [-0.45, -0.25, 'O'], tx); b.create('text', [7.7, 0.5, 'A'], tx); b.create('text', [2.45, 5.2, 'B'], tx);
  function at(P, Q, t) { return [P[0] + t*(Q[0]-P[0]), P[1] + t*(Q[1]-P[1])]; }
  // P on OA and Q on OB at fixed positions (labels only)
  var P = at(O, A, 0.4), Q = at(O, B, 0.4);
  b.create('point', P, po); b.create('point', Q, po);
  b.create('segment', [P, Q], {strokeColor: '#000000', strokeWidth: 1.5, dash: 2, fixed: true, highlight: false});
  b.create('text', [P[0] - 0.1, P[1] - 0.35, 'P'], tx); b.create('text', [Q[0] - 0.45, Q[1] + 0.1, 'Q'], tx);

})();
[[/jsxgraph]]
</div><div>(a) \(\overrightarrow{OP} =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><div>(b) \(\overrightarrow{PQ} =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><div>(c) \(\overrightarrow{PQ} =\) [[input:ans3]] \(\overrightarrow{AB}\) (a fraction) [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(d) What does this show?</p><div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) OP is \({@m@}\) of the \({@m+n@}\) equal parts of OA, so \(\overrightarrow{OP} = \dfrac{ {@m@} }{ {@m+n@} }\,\mathbf{a}\). In the same way \(\overrightarrow{OQ} = \dfrac{ {@m@} }{ {@m+n@} }\,\mathbf{b}\).</p><p>(b) \(\overrightarrow{PQ} = \overrightarrow{OQ} - \overrightarrow{OP} = {@ta2@}\).</p><p>(c) \(\overrightarrow{AB} = \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}\), so \(\overrightarrow{PQ} = \dfrac{ {@m@} }{ {@m+n@} }(\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) = \dfrac{ {@m@} }{ {@m+n@} }\,\overrightarrow{AB}\).</p><p>(d) \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\) is a positive multiple of \(\overrightarrow{AB}\), so PQ is parallel to AB and \(\dfrac{ {@m@} }{ {@m+n@} }\) of its length. PQ and AB do not share a point, so P, Q, A and B are not collinear.[[if test="is(m = n)"]] With P and Q the midpoints, this is the midpoint theorem.[[/if]]</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
